//   Rigidbody 2D ▸ Freeze Rotation Z  =  ON        <- solid hits impart spin
//   Waypoint A / Waypoint B           =  two empty GameObjects placed in the scene`,
  finalNote:
    'Compare against your own file rather than copying it. The gap is the turn, and it is the one line in this lesson with nothing earlier to copy: the field remembers which end of the beat the enemy is heading for, and the turn is that field changing its mind. This file writes it as a small method with an `if`/`else`, and turns *before* it walks where the walkthrough turns after — on every frame but the one where the enemy arrives, those two orders are the same file, and any form that hands the field the *other* waypoint is correct. Your waypoint placement, your speed, your threshold and your movement method from 2.2 are yours; the shape is what has to match.',
};

const body = `<h1>4.1 — The Enemy: Patrol</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The turret never moves. The enemy hunts — and a hunt starts with something calmer: a guard
    walking a beat, back and forth, forever, with no marker in the scene to walk it against.
    Today you decide what "back and forth" actually means, give the enemy the body it does not
    have, and build the calmest half of the machine you will finish in 4.5.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I make an enemy follow a patrol route?', 2)}

${renderDecompose('4.1', DECOMPOSE, 2)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Problem: A Guard With No Memory</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Select <code>Assets/Prefabs/MobileEnemy</code> and read its Inspector. Notice what it has
        — a Transform, an empty <code>EnemyController</code> component, and a child object —
        and then notice what it does <em>not</em> have. Compare it against the Player, which has
        carried a collider and a body since <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a>. Write down the difference
        before you read on.
    </p>

    <p>
        Now the design question. <strong>An enemy that walks back and forth forever must remember
        something.</strong> A player who is not pressing keys simply stops; an enemy left alone
        has to keep going and know when to turn. So: what is the smallest amount of state that
        produces a beat? Work it out before you look at the options — the answer is smaller than
        you think.
    </p>

    <p>Here are the three honest ways to define where a beat begins and ends:</p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>How the beat is defined</th><th>What the enemy stores</th><th>How the turn happens</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>A leash from where it spawned</strong></td>
            <td>Two facts and nothing else: the position it started at, and how far it is allowed to wander.</td>
            <td>Measure how far it has drifted from that anchor — <code>Vector3.Distance(transform.position, anchorPosition)</code>, the same call you used on the turret in <a href="3.1.html">3.1</a> — and when the distance exceeds the leash, flip the stored sign: <code>direction = -direction;</code></td>
            <td>No scene setup whatsoever, and the beat follows the enemy wherever you drag it. But the beat is always centred on the spawn point and is always a straight line — it cannot follow a corridor around a corner. The turn is also only noticed <em>after</em> the enemy has moved, so it always overshoots the limit by up to one frame of travel.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Two placed waypoints</strong></td>
            <td>Two Transforms — usually two empty objects, wired into two Inspector slots.</td>
            <td>Walk toward the current waypoint; when you are close enough, swap which one is the target: <code>if (Vector3.Distance(transform.position, target.position) &lt; 0.1f) { target = other; }</code></td>
            <td>You must create, place and wire the objects, and invent an "arrived" threshold that is small enough to be accurate but not so small the enemy jitters — if one frame of travel is longer than that threshold, the enemy walks straight past the waypoint and never turns at all. In exchange, the beat can be any shape: corners, long routes, a loop of three or more points.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Absolute bounds</strong></td>
            <td>Two numbers: a minimum and a maximum.</td>
            <td>If the position passes either bound, reverse — <code>if (transform.position.y &gt; topLimit) { direction = -1; }</code></td>
            <td>The simplest maths of the three, and the most brittle. Those bounds are world coordinates: move the enemy in the scene and its beat stays behind. Drop a copy of the prefab somewhere else and it will turn on the very first frame. The new value also has to be one the sign is allowed to hold: the moment a bug lets it become <code>0</code>, the enemy stops walking and the beat is over.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>The round IS the behaviour.</strong> Nobody is watching a guard on a beat, and that
        is exactly why the walk has to be worth watching on its own: a beat that reads as a twitch
        looks like a bug, and a beat the player can predict reads as a guard. No line of code
        decides which of those you get — the speed, the length of the beat and any pause at each
        end do. The animation you build in <a href="4.2.html">4.2</a> is only ever animating the
        beat you write today.
    </div>

    <p>
        Whichever you choose, the enemy is still a picture. Before it can be stopped by a wall —
        or touch the player at all in <a href="4.4.html">4.4</a> — you have to give it a body.
        Here is what your options cost:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Body setup for the enemy</th><th>What the player experiences</th><th>What it requires of your code</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>No collider, no Rigidbody2D</strong> — what it ships with</td>
            <td>The enemy walks through walls, through the player, through everything. It is a ghost.</td>
            <td>Nothing. And that is the problem: no collision messages will ever fire on it, so the contact damage in 4.4 becomes impossible to build.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Collider + Dynamic Rigidbody2D, Gravity Scale 0</strong></td>
            <td>The enemy is solid and is stopped by walls — but only if your movement respects physics.</td>
            <td>You must use a movement method from the <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a> table that consults the physics engine. Gravity Scale must be zero, exactly as you set it on the top-down player.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Collider + Kinematic Rigidbody2D</strong></td>
            <td>Completely stable — no gravity, no tumbling, no drift — but the walls of your scene are not solid to <em>it</em>. A Kinematic body only collides with Dynamic bodies, so it patrols straight through every static collider on the map and nothing can push it.</td>
            <td>Kinematic bodies ignore forces and velocity, so you must move it with <code>MovePosition</code> or by transform — and <code>MovePosition</code> will not stop it at a wall either. With no wall able to turn it, the whole beat has to be enforced in your code.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>The enemy's visible sprite is on a child object.</strong> The MobileEnemy root
        carries no Sprite Renderer — the art lives on a child called <em>Sprite</em>, offset
        slightly and scaled up. A default collider added to the <em>root</em> is sized from
        nothing and will not cover the enemy you can see. This is the same trap as the Coin in
        <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a>, and it is worth pressing Play and watching a bullet sail
        through the visible body once, so you never make the mistake twice.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Designing a Beat</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Which of the three beat definitions fits an enemy you will place four or five times across a map and keep nudging while you design the level? What does your choice cost you?</li>
<li>The leash approach stores the position the enemy started at. Which lifecycle method must save it, and what would the enemy do if you saved it every frame instead?</li>
<li>A single int holding <code>1</code> or <code>-1</code> is the enemy's entire memory of where it is going. Why is that enough — and what extra state would a bool such as <code>isGoingRight</code> force you to keep in sync?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

