<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs: The API for a Beat</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Every row below is a call or a declaration you have already typed somewhere in this
        course. Read the last column as carefully as the middle one: the rows with no surprises
        in them are the rows whose costs you have already met once, and the costs are the whole
        reason this lesson is a decision rather than a recipe.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>API</th><th>What it gives you</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>[SerializeField] private Transform waypoint;</code></td>
            <td>A slot in the Inspector holding a reference to another object. An empty GameObject's Transform is a place in the scene, so one slot can hold one end of a beat. The same declaration form you have used since <a href="1.3.html">1.3</a>.</td>
            <td>An empty slot does not complain when you press Play — it throws at the moment your code reads it, which looks like a patrol bug and is really a wiring bug. And a slot belongs to the object it sits on: wired on the prefab, all four enemies share one beat; wired on an instance, that one differs.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Vector3.Distance(a, b)</code></td>
            <td>The straight-line distance between two points — the call that decided whether the turret could see you in <a href="3.1.html">3.1</a>, and now the call that decides whether the enemy has arrived.</td>
            <td>It measures <em>between two positions</em>, so both have to exist. Ask it about a <code>null</code> waypoint and the script stops with a NullReferenceException — one every frame, in the Console, pointing at that line.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>(a - b).normalized</code></td>
            <td>Subtracting two positions gives the arrow from one to the other; normalizing throws its length away and leaves pure direction. The diagonal fix from <a href="1.5.html">1.5</a>, reused to aim a projectile in <a href="3.2.html">3.2</a>.</td>
            <td>Miss it out and the arrow is as long as the distance, so "direction × speed" quietly becomes a speed that grows with the gap. The enemy sprints when it is far from the waypoint and never arrives cleanly — and nothing about the line looks wrong.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Rigidbody2D.linearVelocity = v</code></td>
            <td>Hand the movement to the physics engine — your movement method from <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a>, and the only reason a wall can stop the enemy at all.</td>
            <td>It is measured in units <em>per second</em>, so it takes no <code>Time.deltaTime</code>; add one and the enemy's speed starts depending on your frame rate. Unity 6 renamed this from <code>velocity</code>, so tutorials written before then will not compile.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>transform.position</code></td>
            <td>Where the enemy is right now — one half of every distance you measure, and the thing the waypoints are compared against.</td>
            <td>Reading it is free. <em>Writing</em> to it is the trap: it moves the object without telling the physics engine, which is exactly how the Player walked through the level in <a href="1.4.html">1.4</a>. When a body owns the movement, nothing else may write the position.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>All three approaches are the same four parts in different clothes.</strong> A body
        a wall can stop, one field that remembers which way the enemy is walking, one movement
        line that consults the physics engine, and one distance check that changes that field. The
        leash calls the field a sign; the waypoints call it a target; the bounds call it a limit.
        The walkthrough below builds the waypoint version, and if you chose a different row you are
        still building those four parts — the differences between the rows are exactly what the
        table above priced.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
<li>The leash row measures a distance that grows and shrinks forever. If you flip the sign the moment the drift exceeds the leash, the enemy is already <em>past</em> the limit when it turns. How far past — and what decides that?</li>
<li>The waypoint row compares a distance against a small threshold. What goes wrong if you set that threshold to zero, and what goes wrong if you set it to five on a two-metre route?</li>
<li>The bounds row reverses using a stored sign. What are the only two values that sign may hold, and what happens if a bug lets it become zero?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

