${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Give It a Beat</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 1 — find your enemies.</strong> The Overworld scene already contains
        <strong>four</strong> MobileEnemy instances. Count them yourself in the Hierarchy before
        you add anything, and note where each one sits. There is exactly <strong>one</strong> Coin
        in that scene. You may move the enemies, and you may drag more from
        <code>Assets/Prefabs</code> if your design needs them — but count first, so you know what
        you actually changed.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Step 2 — give it a body.</strong> Add the collider and the body you chose to the
        MobileEnemy prefab, and set Gravity Scale deliberately. Then press Play with no patrol
        code at all and walk into it. Find out whether it is solid yet.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Step 3 — build your beat.</strong> The walkthrough above built the waypoint pair,
        step by step. If that is the row you chose, your remaining work is the waypoints
        themselves: create the two objects, wire the slots, and tune the speed and the threshold
        against the size of the Overworld. If you chose the leash or the bounds, this is where you
        build <em>yours</em> — the five decisions below are the same five for every row, and the
        answers look nothing alike.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification for <code>EnemyController.cs</code>:</strong>

        <p><strong>The body — whichever beat you chose:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Which two components does the prefab need before a wall can stop the enemy, and which of the two does the script need before it can hear <em>anything</em> from the physics engine? Which object do they go on?</li>
            <li>The visible enemy is not on the root. How will you size the collider against the art you can see, and how do you check that in the Scene view rather than by guessing?</li>
            <li>What Body Type and what Gravity Scale did you set, and which of the two decides whether a wall stops the enemy at all?</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>The ends of the beat:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>What does your row store — a spawn anchor plus a distance, two waypoint Transforms, or two numbers? What does each of those cost you when you duplicate the enemy across the map?</li>
            <li>If you chose the leash, what does the anchor <em>mean</em> to a designer moving the enemy around the scene? Say it in one sentence a level designer would understand.</li>
            <li>If you chose waypoints, where do the two empty objects live — children of the enemy, or separate objects in the scene? What breaks about each choice when you duplicate the enemy?</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>The memory — one field, and the whole beat hangs off it:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>What single field records which way the enemy is walking? What type is smallest for your row, and what value does it start at?</li>
            <li>If your row stores a sign, which arithmetic operation reverses it, and why is that cheaper than an <code>if</code> that assigns the opposite value? What are the only two values it may ever hold?</li>
            <li>If your row stores a waypoint, how does the turn choose the other one — and what is left of the beat if that line keeps writing the same one?</li>
            <li>Whichever your row: where does that field live, inside <code>Update()</code> or in the class body? Say what breaks if you get it wrong, using the word "frame" in your answer.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>The movement:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Which movement call do you use — the one you chose for the player in <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a>, or a different one? Whatever you pick, how do you keep the speed frame-rate independent?</li>
            <li>A top-down enemy could patrol on either axis. Which axis does your design use, and what single character in your movement line decides that?</li>
            <li>What has to be true of the enemy's body before your movement line has anything to write to, and where is that lookup done — once, or every frame?</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>The turn:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Which measurement decides that the beat has ended, and which of the three rows does it belong to?</li>
            <li>Which comparison operator do you use at the boundary — and what would the enemy do at exactly the boundary with the other one?</li>
            <li>What number decides "close enough"? What happens if it is smaller than the distance the enemy travels in a single frame?</li>
            <li>How do you report each turn to the Console so you can watch the beat without a UI?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <p>
        Save and press Play. Watch one full beat end to end. The enemy should walk one way, turn,
        walk back, and repeat — forever, with no input from you.
    </p>

</div>

